## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a deadline. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout
            task.Wait(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }

        // Propagate any work exception
        if (task.IsFaulted)
            throw new AggregateException(task.Exception!.InnerExceptions);

        return task.Result;
    }
}
```

### If the `work` delegate is long-running, accept a token:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

Work must cooperate by calling `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints.

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints. Long CPU-bound work without `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` won't stop. |
| **Blocking calls ignore the token** | `Thread.Sleep`, raw socket reads, etc. won't respond. Use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` or async I/O instead. |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers. Always wrap in `using`. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the orchestration boundary (here, to convert to `TimeoutException`). |
| **Uncooperative third-party code** | If you can't modify the work to check a token, move it to a child **process** and use `Process.Kill()` — this is the only true "abort" in modern .NET. |

### What Was Removed

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Replacement |
|--------------------|-------------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` (via timeout) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed — not needed with cooperative model |

### References

- [SYSLIB0006 – Thread.Abort obsolete](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/fundamentals/syslib-diagnostics/syslib0006)
- [Cancellation in managed threads](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/standard/threading/cancellation-in-managed-threads)
